WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.700
A macrophage swallows the equivalent of its whole surface about every half hour, and

2
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:10.700
it does not shrink.

3
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:14.660
Every receptor in that membrane makes the trip too.

4
00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.500
At the surface, the tail of a receptor points into the cell.

5
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.040
So where does the receptor go once it is inside?

6
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.620
And what does the trip do to its signal?

7
00:00:26.483 --> 00:00:33.143
A receptor enters a pit because its tail carries a sequence that an adaptor can

8
00:00:33.143 --> 00:00:37.923
read. The adaptor links the tail to a coat of clathrin.

9
00:00:38.803 --> 00:00:42.203
The pit deepens until it hangs by a thin neck.

10
00:00:43.263 --> 00:00:47.383
A protein called dynamin builds a collar around the neck.

11
00:00:47.383 --> 00:00:53.423
When it breaks down GTP, the neck is cut and the vesicle is free.

12
00:00:55.217 --> 00:01:01.257
The vesicle reaches the early endosome, the first stop for almost all cargo that is

13
00:01:01.257 --> 00:01:07.157
taken in. On the outer membrane, the tail still points into the cytosol and can

14
00:01:07.157 --> 00:01:08.417
still reach its partners.

15
00:01:08.657 --> 00:01:11.617
So a receptor can go on signaling from here.

16
00:01:12.877 --> 00:01:16.977
A receptor with no other instruction spreads into thin tubes.

17
00:01:17.797 --> 00:01:21.697
The tubes pinch off, and that receptor mostly goes back to the surface.

18
00:01:22.617 --> 00:01:26.857
A receptor leaves that default route when it carries a tag.

19
00:01:27.597 --> 00:01:29.257
The tag is ubiquitin.

20
00:01:30.137 --> 00:01:35.257
For an active EGF receptor, an enzyme called Cbl adds it.

21
00:01:36.057 --> 00:01:40.637
A set of proteins on the endosome, called ESCRT, binds the tag.

22
00:01:41.437 --> 00:01:44.017
They gather the tagged receptors into a patch.

23
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:51.030
ESCRT pushes the patch inward until it pinches off as a small vesicle inside the

24
00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:54.690
endosome. Now ask which way the tail faces.

25
00:01:55.630 --> 00:02:00.810
Inside that vesicle, the tail is sealed in and cannot reach the cytosol at all.

26
00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:04.370
Its partners are out of reach, so the signal ends.

27
00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:10.170
That happens before the lysosome, a bag of digestive enzymes, destroys anything.

28
00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:12.873
Now break the sorting.

29
00:02:13.773 --> 00:02:19.893
In some lung cancers, the receptor MET loses the docking site for the enzyme that

30
00:02:19.893 --> 00:02:25.893
adds ubiquitin. Poorly tagged, it escapes the lysosome and keeps signaling for

31
00:02:25.893 --> 00:02:26.933
longer than it should.

32
00:02:27.693 --> 00:02:32.533
The fault is not an overactive kinase, but a receptor that misses its turn.

33
00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:39.713
So keep this rule: taken in, a receptor can still signal, but sealed in, it cannot.
